<header><p class="st-kicker">electorate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Electorate of Württemberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1803 CE – 1806 CE · capital Stuttgart</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Württemberg's brief rank as an electorate between the duchy and the kingdom, a step in Frederick I's rise under Napoleon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-wurttemberg">Frederick II of Württemberg</a> lost his lands at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-montbeliard">Montbéliard</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> but gained secularised church lands and imperial cities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a>. As part of the same settlement he was made an elector of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Frederick used his new rank to centralise power, overriding the traditional estates, which had long limited the dukes. He added former imperial cities such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-heilbronn">Heilbronn</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-reutlingen">Reutlingen</a> and Catholic lands like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellwangen">Ellwangen</a>, which made the Protestant state more mixed in religion. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1805 and sent troops to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Austerlitz</a> campaign.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-pressburg-1805">Peace of Pressburg</a> rewarded Frederick with further territory and recognition as a sovereign king. On 1 January 1806 he proclaimed himself King of Württemberg, and the electorate gave way to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/electorate-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Electorate of Württemberg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Oinkuss · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_W%C3%BCrttemberg_(1806_-_1816).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1803 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>electorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stuttgart</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Swabian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kurfürstentum Württemberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=electorate-of-wurttemberg" aria-label="Electorate of Württemberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4892 474 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Electorate of Württemberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5212 730L5212 730M5212 730L5229 694M5212 730L5212 730M5212 730L5234 725M5212 730L5220 746"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5212 730h0M5229 694h0M5212 730h0M5234 725h0M5220 746h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5212 730h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Stuttgart. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=electorate-of-wurttemberg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
